Karray, Martin Takac (MBZUAI, TUM), arXiv:2610.02478.
Code: https://github.com/machinestein/Tropical-Reinforcement-Learning, commit 75106338f320a4c093ea7c1fcfc9fcfc6a5acd20,
MIT licence (notice below).

What is taken, and what changed:
  * countdown.py ports ragen/env/countdown/generate.py (the problem generator, unchanged, same seeds) and the step
    semantics of ragen/env/countdown/tropic_env.py and state.py (one exact binary operation per step, the state as the
    target plus the sorted multiset of live numbers). Changed: a lenient answer parser (the first "a op b" in the last
    <answer> tag, else in the reply), the same for every agent, because an untrained 0.5B model rarely answers in
    TROPIC's strict format; an independent expression verifier was added.
  * The step graph, the L-best prefix/suffix recursion in the max-plus semiring, candidates(), certify(),
    choose_frontier() and select_basis() follow ragen/tropic/graph.py; admit() and the restart rule follow
    ragen/tropic/collector.py; export_tropic() weights follow ragen/tropic/batch.py (basis_rows). Changed: edges are
    environment steps (state, action, next state) that several contributors can file, with their provenance; frontier
    choice skips dead ends instead of falling back to the root; a fixed scorer (the base model with one prompt, on a
    canonical "<answer>a op b</answer>" completion) replaces TROPIC's rescoring under the current actor, because no
    training happens here; and the agent's prompt holds the current state only, as in TROPIC's single-turn mode.
  * TROPIC's instruction text for Countdown (config/_4_countdown_tropic.yaml) is used verbatim as agent A's system
    prompt and as the scorer's prompt.

